WEBVTT

00:00:04.381 --> 00:00:11.381
Thank you very much.

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:19.612
Hello, I don't think we've met. Sam Eriks.

00:00:20.482 --> 00:00:22.822
Victor Tang. Pleased to meet you.

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:24.962
And what company are you from Mr Tang?

00:00:24.962 --> 00:00:28.002
OceanWide. I'm the sales representative
for this region.

00:00:28.002 --> 00:00:32.012
Ah yes. I know your company. Your business

00:00:32.012 --> 00:00:33.852
is expanding very rapidly at the moment.

00:00:33.852 --> 00:00:37.982
Yes, we're doing quite well. And yourself?
Who do you work for?

00:00:37.982 --> 00:00:40.632
Actually I work for myself. I'm the C.E.O.

00:00:40.632 --> 00:00:44.611
of a small export and packaging company. We
specialise in seafood.

00:00:44.611 --> 00:00:46.202
It's a growing market.

00:00:46.202 --> 00:00:49.491
Yes, but a very competitive one Mr Tang.

00:00:49.491 --> 00:00:51.901
Please call me Victor.

00:00:51.901 --> 00:00:55.142
Sam. Victor, let me introduce you to someone.

00:00:55.142 --> 00:00:59.812
Lin this is Victor Tang he's the regional rep. for OceanWide. This is Lin

00:00:59.812 --> 00:01:01.411
Chan, my sales manager.

00:01:01.411 --> 00:01:02.581
How do you do Mr Tang?

00:01:02.581 --> 00:01:05.221
I'm very well thank you. Nice to meet you
Ms Chan.

00:01:05.221 --> 00:01:07.182
And you.

00:01:07.182 --> 00:01:12.441
Can I get you another drink?

00:01:12.441 --> 00:01:17.121
Thank you.

00:01:17.121 --> 00:01:20.801
Let's look at introductions. First how

00:01:20.801 --> 00:01:25.151
did Sam introduce himself to Victor who
he didn't know?

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:28.331
Hello, I don't think we've met.

00:01:28.331 --> 00:01:29.272
Sam Eriks.

00:01:29.272 --> 00:01:30.411
Victor Tang.

00:01:30.411 --> 00:01:31.882
Pleased to meet you.

00:01:31.882 --> 00:01:38.882
Sam said hello. We can say hello in most situations.
He also said I don't think we've met. This

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:49.001
is a good phrase to use if you want to meet
someone new. Practise saying Hello, I don't

00:01:49.121 --> 00:01:56.121
think we've met, and then say your name.
Hello. I don't think we've met

00:01:56.422 --> 00:01:57.481
Victor Tang.

00:01:57.481 --> 00:02:02.482
Pleased to meet you.
Victor replied by saying his name and pleased

00:02:02.482 --> 00:02:09.482
to meet you. Pleased to meet you is a good
formal greeting for most situations. After

00:02:10.172 --> 00:02:15.542
Sam introduces himself, say your name, and
'pleased to meet you'.

00:02:15.542 --> 00:02:18.652
Hello, I don't think we've met.
Sam Eriks.

00:02:18.652 --> 00:02:23.032
Pleased to meet you.

00:02:23.032 --> 00:02:28.862
And the Western tradition is to shake hands
when you meet someone. Usually, when we first

00:02:28.862 --> 00:02:34.282
meet someone in a business situation, we want
to find out what they do - what their job,

00:02:34.282 --> 00:02:39.922
or position is. Let's see how Victor and Sam
do this.

00:02:39.922 --> 00:02:41.942
And what company are you from Mr Tang?

00:02:41.942 --> 00:02:44.882
OceanWide. I'm the sales representative for
this region.

00:02:44.882 --> 00:02:50.282
Ah yes. I know your company.
Your business is expanding very rapidly at

00:02:50.282 --> 00:02:50.732
the moment.

00:02:50.732 --> 00:02:54.862
Yes, we're doing quite well.
And yourself? Who do you work for?

00:02:54.862 --> 00:02:57.682
Actually I work for myself. I'm the C.E.O.

00:02:57.682 --> 00:03:02.022
of a small export and packaging company. We
specialise in seafood.

00:03:02.022 --> 00:03:08.702
Sam asks and what company are you from? There
are a few different ways you could ask this

00:03:08.702 --> 00:03:15.702
question: Let's try some of them:
What company are you from?

00:03:16.852 --> 00:03:21.662
Which company do you represent?

00:03:21.662 --> 00:03:28.662
What's your line of business?

00:03:29.602 --> 00:03:35.071
Now you try answering Sam's question with
your own answer, saying what company you are

00:03:35.071 --> 00:03:38.152
from...
And what company are you from?

00:03:38.152 --> 00:03:43.102
Ah yes, I know that company.

00:03:43.102 --> 00:03:48.792
And you can be more specific by asking about
someone's job. Practise these...

00:03:48.792 --> 00:03:53.002
And what's your position there?

00:03:53.002 --> 00:04:00.002
And what do you do there?
Position means the same as job, but it's a

00:04:00.711 --> 00:04:07.711
more formal term. Listen to how both Sam and
Victor describe their positions:

00:04:08.182 --> 00:04:11.501
I'm the sales representative for this region.

00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:16.471
I'm the C.E.O. of a small export and packaging
company.

00:04:16.471 --> 00:04:23.471
We describe our job by saying I am or I'm
and then naming the position. Notice that

00:04:23.761 --> 00:04:30.761
Sam says he is the C.E.O. or Chief Executive
Officer of his company. When someone asks

00:04:32.041 --> 00:04:39.041
who you work for, it can be useful to also
tell them what your job is. When meeting someone,

00:04:39.342 --> 00:04:45.492
it helps them if you offer information before
they ask. This makes the conversation more

00:04:45.492 --> 00:04:52.492
relaxed. Now Sam is going to ask you who you
work for and what your position is - answer

00:04:52.842 --> 00:04:57.992
his questions with your own answers:
And what company are you from?

00:04:58.001 --> 00:05:02.701
I'm from...
Ah yes, I know that company.

00:05:02.701 --> 00:05:06.511
And what's your position there?
I'm the...

00:05:06.511 --> 00:05:13.511
Oh really. That's interesting.
Now, the business of names. Should we use

00:05:13.712 --> 00:05:20.712
formal titles, such as Mr, Mrs or Ms, or should
we use informal, more familiar names. Let's

00:05:22.231 --> 00:05:25.681
see how Victor solves this problem:
It's a growing market.

00:05:25.681 --> 00:05:29.011
Yes, but a very competitive one Mr Tang.

00:05:29.011 --> 00:05:31.812
Please call me Victor.

00:05:31.812 --> 00:05:32.751
Sam.

00:05:32.751 --> 00:05:39.751
Sam uses Victor's formal title - Mr Tang.
But Victor says Please call me Victor. Now

00:05:40.661 --> 00:05:45.951
that they have met, it's more comfortable
for them to use each other's first names - at

00:05:45.951 --> 00:05:52.951
least in this less formal situation. Practise
saying Please call me, and your first name

00:05:53.742 --> 00:05:58.631
after Sam's statement.
Yes, but a very competitive one Mr Tang.

00:05:58.631 --> 00:06:05.631
Please call me...
Sam introduces Victor to someone else. Let's

00:06:06.131 --> 00:06:10.082
watch how he does this.
Victor, let me introduce you to someone.

00:06:10.082 --> 00:06:13.511
Lin - this is Victor Tang - he's the regional

00:06:13.511 --> 00:06:14.731
rep. for OceanWide.

00:06:14.731 --> 00:06:16.911
This is Lin Chan, my sales manager.

00:06:16.911 --> 00:06:18.171
How do you do Mr Tang?

00:06:18.171 --> 00:06:21.411
I'm very well thank you.
Nice to meet you Ms Chan.

00:06:21.411 --> 00:06:28.411
Sam says let me introduce you to someone.
This is a very useful phrase. Practise saying

00:06:28.962 --> 00:06:35.962
it after Sam:
Let me introduce you to someone.

00:06:36.441 --> 00:06:42.492
When Sam introduces Lin, he has four pieces
of information. Listen carefully to what they

00:06:42.492 --> 00:06:45.712
are:
This is Victor Tang, he's the regional

00:06:45.712 --> 00:06:46.932
rep. for OceanWide.

00:06:46.932 --> 00:06:52.902
This is Lin Chan, my sales manager.
Did you hear the four parts of Sam's introduction?

00:06:52.902 --> 00:06:58.631
First, he told Lin Victor's full name. Second,

00:06:58.631 --> 00:07:05.601
he told her what Victor's position was and
his company name. Third, he introduced Lin

00:07:05.601 --> 00:07:12.601
using her full name, and finally he told Victor
what Lin's job was. This way both Victor and

00:07:14.212 --> 00:07:20.231
Lin know enough about each other to start
a conversation. When introducing people at

00:07:20.231 --> 00:07:24.551
a function, it's important to try to make
them feel comfortable.

00:07:24.551 --> 00:07:29.941
Finally, listen again to how Lin and Victor

00:07:29.941 --> 00:07:32.691
greet each other:
How do you do Mr Tang?

00:07:32.691 --> 00:07:34.152
I'm very well thank you.

00:07:34.152 --> 00:07:35.621
Nice to meet you Ms Chan.

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:40.641
And you.
Lin uses the phrase how do you do. Practise

00:07:40.641 --> 00:07:44.492
this phrase:
How do you do?

00:07:44.492 --> 00:07:49.281
I'm very well thank you.
How do you do is another formal phrase for

00:07:49.281 --> 00:07:56.281
introductions. Victor replies with a formal
phrase too I'm very well thankyou. Practise

00:07:57.921 --> 00:08:02.631
this after Lin's question.
How do you do Mr Tang?

00:08:02.631 --> 00:08:08.731
I'm very well thank you.
If someone says How do you do? or How are

00:08:08.731 --> 00:08:15.701
you? we usually say I'm very well thankyou.And
we could ask them how they are, by saying

00:08:15.701 --> 00:08:19.631
How do you do? or How are you?

00:08:19.631 --> 00:08:24.322
Let's review some of the phrases we've learnt
today, for introducing yourself and other

00:08:24.322 --> 00:08:30.712
people. Practise the phrases with our characters
Sam, Victor and Lin.

00:08:30.712 --> 00:08:35.011
Hello, I don't think we've met.

00:08:35.011 --> 00:08:39.331
And what company are you from?

00:08:39.332 --> 00:08:43.932
Let me introduce you to someone.

00:08:43.932 --> 00:08:49.512
This is Lin Chan, my sales manager.

00:08:49.512 --> 00:08:53.432
Pleased to meet you.

00:08:53.432 --> 00:08:57.512
Who do you work for?

00:08:57.512 --> 00:09:01.102
Please call me Victor.

00:09:01.102 --> 00:09:05.252
I'm very well thank you.

00:09:05.252 --> 00:09:08.102
How do you do Mr Tang?

00:09:08.102 --> 00:09:14.912
Nice to meet you.
Remember, when meeting people, tell them something

00:09:14.912 --> 00:09:18.452
about yourself before being asked.

00:09:18.452 --> 00:09:23.552
When you ask people questions about themselves
in English, use a falling tone - it sounds

00:09:23.552 --> 00:09:28.562
friendlier. Instead of Who do you work for?
(upward inflection)

00:09:28.562 --> 00:09:31.162
Who do you work for? (downward inflection)

00:09:31.162 --> 00:09:36.152
And when introducing a colleague, or someone
you've met, use the full names of both people,

00:09:36.152 --> 00:09:38.602
and their positions.

00:09:38.602 --> 00:09:43.082
And that's all for today on The Business of
English. I'll see you next time.